WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.840
Film Courage: Red flags of a film distribution
deal?

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.720
Mark Heidelberger, producer: Red flags of
a distribution deal?

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.689
You saved one of the toughest [questions]
for last.

00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:08.200
Film Courage: Sorry.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.809
Mark: Actually maybe not.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:15.440
So a lot of the pictures I do are home entertainment
titles.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.350
They are not necessarily pictures that will
go to theatrical.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.359
If they do they’ll go limited theatrical.

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:28.340
And I think a lot of times when I look at
a contract, I’m looking at the marketing

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.130
and how much they are saying the marketing
is going to cost.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:37.360
A lot of the distributors that I try to avoid
are these guys that put in the contracts that

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.570
well they’re going to spend $50,000 or $75,000
or $100,000 on P&amp;A [Prints and Advertising]

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:42.570
and Marketing.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.390
And I’m like Well what exactly are you going
to spend that money on?”

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.770
Because to get the guy (the average guy) just
walking down the street to see your movie,

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.850
like you need 5 million dollars minimum.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.410
I mean you need huge amount of P&amp;A just to
get that random guy walking down the street

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.100
to even know what your movie is.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:02.890
The amount of money it takes to market, billboards
and bus shelters, and bus sides and TV spots

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.600
and online and getting that banner on IMDB
and front page…it costs millions and millions

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.490
of dollars to do a campaign to literally draw
those kinds of eyeballs.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.290
Home entertainment is such a different beast.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.900
A lot of it is done now through social media,
free publicity and getting junkets with your

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.850
actors or getting reviews written up and that
sort of thing.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:30.280
Or for other creative, non-expensive forms
of marketing for instance going onto Xbox

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.480
or Netflix that says “You might also like…”

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:38.900
Getting high up on that algorithm where you
film is listed with some big “You might

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.980
also like this little film…”

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:49.010
That means so much more to a smaller film
or just getting an end cap in Walmart is huge.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.450
It may not cost you a lot but if you have
a distributor that has a relationship that

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.690
can do that, that’s what happened with NINJA
APOCALYPSE, we got an end cap in Walmart and

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:03.860
and in Best Buy and that didn’t cost anything
but that was sort of smart, a distributor

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:04.860
using relationships and that sort of thing.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:11.510
That’s how you get your premiering in a
festival that’s got a really good promotion,

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.709
sponsorship and press behind it.

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:17.210
We premiered NINJA APOCALYPSE at Comic-Con
in San Diego.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:22.370
So instead of spending millions and millions
of dollars it’s more things like that I

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.370
look for.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.620
So when I see a distribution contract that
says I’m going to spend $75,000 on marketing,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.620
I’m like “On what?

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.620
What are you spending it on?”

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.280
And they are like “Well, I’ve got to cut
trailers.”

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.600
And I’m like “I can cut trailers.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:32.600
I can get a trailer cut for a grand.”

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.520
And they’re like “Well we’ve got to
get new posters.”

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:36.520
I’m like “Same thing.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.620
I’ve got a designer that can get new posters.”

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.690
I would rather do those things and instead
of having the distributor bill some tremendous

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:50.760
mark up and a lot of times if they have that
$75,000 marketing expense built in you are

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.440
not going to see any of that money until that
$75,000 is recouped.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.240
It’s not like they are going to say “Well,
I’ve only spent $10,000 of it so we’ll

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.240
start cutting you checks now.”

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.270
No, there are going to say “We haven’t
hit that $75,000 mark yet.”

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:09.780
So I really look at that number (how much
they are spending) and it should be a smaller

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.080
number and honestly want to know what it is
where we actually have a chance at making

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.750
some getting some recoupment here, not you
have to make $75,000 or a $100,000 before

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:23.300
you make see first dollar because on a million
dollar picture you are not going to be out

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:31.080
there in theaters making 30 million dollars
so the economic are different and how that

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.410
money is spent is different.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:40.690
So things like going to film markets, that’s
a reasonable, justifiable distribution expense.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:49.480
And then marketing expenses, there are certain…cutting
trailers can be reasonable as long as I know

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.702
what they are spending on it.

00:03:50.702 --> 00:03:55.850
And I have the ability to approve any expense
over a certain amount so I know they are going

00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:02.230
to be reasonable and you know there could
be working meals, lunches with buyers, things

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.560
like that which are reasonable but up to a
certain amount.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.120
So I’m usually looking for that number to
be curtailed somewhat.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:17.190
And then in terms of the term of the distribution
contract you know 7 years is pretty standard,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:22.169
I would almost never do anything in perpetuity.

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:25.880
Sometimes you see contracts that are 10 years,
12 years that might seem excessive.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:31.940
In terms of fees, like for me I’m kind of
looking around the 20% range in terms of what

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.389
I like to pay a distributor.

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:43.039
Some charge as much as 30% or more so red
flags to me are typically terms, fees, marketing

00:04:43.039 --> 00:04:47.389
expenses and then other than that you are
kind of looking at an onerous language that

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:50.569
ties you to something that you don’t want
to be tied to like “Oh, we want rights to

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:52.110
all future sales.”

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:57.199
Like unless you are willing to invest money
in those future sales we are only talking

00:04:57.199 --> 00:05:00.969
about this picture right here.

00:05:00.969 --> 00:05:06.789
So yeah and then I think you can tell reputable
distributors too just by the language in their

00:05:06.789 --> 00:05:07.789
contracts.

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:11.599
A lot of times too if you start to see little
things like if you start to see things like

00:05:11.599 --> 00:05:14.969
typos and spelling mistakes and grammar mistakes
and this is a distributor you are not familiar

00:05:14.969 --> 00:05:18.629
with to me that’s a red flag because any
legitimate distributor is going to have a

00:05:18.629 --> 00:05:22.240
legitimate attorney drafting those contracts
and are going to see those mistakes.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:27.860
I typically don’t deal with distributors
that I’ve never heard of.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:31.599
Usually they come to me by way of someone
I know who has worked with them.

00:05:31.599 --> 00:05:35.669
You know, they’ve been around awhile, I
know their work.

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:39.659
So I start to stick with distributors who
I know have a good reputation, I know people

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:42.770
who have worked with them and say good things
about them so I don’t have to worry about

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.410
a lot of those issues.

